<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Igboland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alaigbo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Igboland is the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo people</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, lying mostly east of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and extending into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> hinterland. It covers forest and savanna country and today spans Anambra, Enugu, Imo, Abia and Ebonyi states and parts of Delta and Rivers. Before colonial rule it was divided among many self-governing communities, linked by the ritual authority of Nri and the trade network of the Aro.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igboland.jpg" alt="Igboland" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NuclearVacuum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igbo_Community_in_Nigeria_and_Africa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Igboland is a cultural and linguistic region, divided by the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> into a larger eastern and a smaller western part. West of the river it reaches Aniocha, Ndokwa, Ukwuani and Ika in present-day Delta State; in the east it ends at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river">Cross River</a>, and in the north it enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsukka">Nsukka</a>. It lies mainly in the lowland forest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a>, Tiv, Yako, Idoma and Ibibio. The Niger has long joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> on both banks, and routes through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaba">Asaba</a>, Onitsha and Aboh carried trade and migrants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a> people were present at least 10,000 years ago, and in 1978 a team led by Thurstan Shaw excavated a rock quarry that had served as a mine for tool and pottery making for a settlement at Ibagwa. The Nsukka-Okigwe axis is proposed as the heartland from which most Igbo-speaking groups spread. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> works found at Igbo Ukwu from 1939, made from local copper by the 9th century, show an industry of local origin, and glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> that originated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were found in a burial there.</p><p>The Kingdom of Nri, said in Umunri tradition to stem from the divine figure Eri, rose around the 10th century. In the 1560s Eze Chima, exiled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-benin">Oba of Benin</a>, founded the western communities known as Umuezechima. The Aro Confederacy, centred on Arochukwu, rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river">Cross River</a> region in the 17th century and built much of its economy on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> to Europeans through coastal middlemen.</p><p>Missionaries arrived from the 1850s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> became the chief export. Igboland was conquered by the British after decades of resistance, among it the Ekumeku Movement, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-aro-war">Anglo-Aro War</a> and the Aba Women's Riots, and British rule set warrant chiefs where no monarchies had existed. After independence in 1960 most of Igboland lay in the Eastern Region, which seceded as the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a> on 30 May 1967 under Emeka Odumegwu-Ojukwu. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigerian-civil-war">Nigerian Civil War</a> lasted from 6 July 1967 to 15 January 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>More than 30 million people live in Igboland, at densities of 140 to 390 inhabitants per square kilometre, over an area of some 40,900 to 41,400 km2. Its cultural subgroups include the Ngwa, the Mbaise, the Ezza, the Etche and the Anioma-Enuani, and Nri is the oldest existing monarchy in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Igboland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Igbo hinterland, ritual sanctions could not protect communities from armed men, and by the eighteenth century the most powerful network in much of <b>Igboland</b> was that of the Aro, who relied on their oracle at Arochukwu and on warriors from allied communities such as Abam, Ohafia and Edda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nri">Kingdom of Nri · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Aro traders founded colonies along trade routes throughout <b>Igboland</b> and beyond, and used the oracle, alliances with mercenary groups such as the Abam, and markets to move slaves to the ports of the Bonny Kingdom and Old Calabar, later dealing in palm oil.</blockquote><a href="/state/aro-confederacy">Aro Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Controlling river traffic, Igala traded in slaves, cloth and salt, and its influence reached into northern <b>Igboland</b> around Nsukka.</blockquote><a href="/state/igala-kingdom">Igala Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Jaja, born in <b>Igboland</b> and sold as a boy to Bonny, rose to head the Anna Pepple trading house of the Bonny Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-opobo">Kingdom of Opobo</a></li></ul></section>
